y[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]creates it, so unrelated agents stop discovering stale rows at the [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]symvision lint gate. Keep the closed-bead symvision refusal and the [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]epic-whitelist policy. Add regression coverage for the reproduced path.[/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  2 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Stop behavior-neutral module splits from turning the TUI [/bold #D7AFFF]     [bold white on #D75F87] large  [/bold white on #D75F87]\n      [bold #D7AFFF]import budget red (sase-1ic)[/bold #D7AFFF]\n    [#AF87FF]import-budget-churn[dim] · [dim][/dim]no dependencies[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]import-budget-churn: the first-party TUI import-count guard in [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]tests/ace/tui/test_app_import_budget.py has been re-raised after toobig [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]splits and reopened twice (+35). Make the guard catch genuine new [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]import-path work while staying neutral to mechanical splits of modules [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]already on the path, without weakening the deferred-module list or just [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]raising the cap again. Verify against recent split commits and a [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]synthetic new eager import.[/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  3 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Fix the sase-core federation_worker socket test flake [/bold #D7AFFF]        [bold white on #D75F87] large  [/bold white on #D75F87]\n      [bold #D7AFFF](sase-1gz)[/bold #D7AFFF]\n    [#AF87FF]core-federation-socket-flake[dim] · [dim][/dim]no dependencies[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]core-federation-socket-flake: in sase-core, reproduce [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]federation_worker::imp::tests::ipc::listener_creates_private_socket_and_[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]rejects_symlink failing under the parallel sase_gateway gate (+15, ten [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]recurrences since 2026-10-18, latest 2026-11-01). Find the shared-state [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]or timing cause (for example temp-dir or socket-path races in listener [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]recovery) and fix it without weakening the private-socket or [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]symlink-rejection assertions. Prove it with a loaded loop.[/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  4 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Fix the zsh sbd alias completion PTY flake (sase-13a)[/bold #D7AFFF]         [bold white on #D75F87] large  [/bold white on #D75F87]\n    [#AF87FF]zsh-completion-pty-flake[dim] · [dim][/dim]no dependencies[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]zsh-completion-pty-flake: reproduce [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]tests/completion/test_zsh_smoke.py::test_alias_sbd_completes_static_bead[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]_tree timing out in loaded full lanes (+15, latest 2026-10-31). Identify[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]whether PTY startup, completion-cache warmup, or shared state causes the[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]stall, and fix the cause without dropping the alias completion assertion[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]or simply raising its timeout.[/#D7D7AF]\n[dim]  5 [bold #AF87FF][/dim]◆ [bold #D7AFFF][/bold #AF87FF]Fix the AGY usage-probe rate-limit timeout flake (sase-19m)[/bold #D7AFFF]   [bold black on #FFD75F] medium [/bold black on #FFD75F]\n    [#AF87FF]agy-probe-timeout-flake[dim] · [dim][/dim]no dependencies[/#AF87FF][/dim]\n    [#D7D7AF]agy-probe-timeout-flake: [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]tests/llm_provider/test_agy_usage_probe.py::test_agy_usage_probe_timeout[/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]_with_rate_limit_stderr_is_rate_limited fails under the loaded [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]diff-scoped lane (timeout classified instead of rate_limited; latest +1 [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]2026-11-01) and passes alone. Make the test and, if needed, the probe [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]classification deterministic under load while keeping the [/#D7D7AF]\n    [#D7D7AF]rate-limit-wins-over-timeout contract.[/#D7D7AF]","clan_tribe":"epic","epic_bead_id":"sase-1qn","llm_provider":"codex","model":"gpt-6-astra","patch_name":"gh_sase-org__sase","phase_bead_id":"s